      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://group.bmj.com/group/rights-licensing/permissions.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To assess the effect of a single dose of intravenous dexamethasone in addition to routine treatment on visual analogue scale (VAS) pain scores at 24\u2005h in emergency department (ED) patients with low back pain with radiculopathy (LBPR)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Double-blind randomised controlled trial of 58 adult ED patients with LBPR, conducted in one tertiary and one urban ED. The intervention was 8\u2005mg of intravenous dexamethasone (or placebo) in addition to current routine care. The primary outcome was the change in VAS pain scores between presentation and 24\u2005h. Secondary outcomes included VAS pain scores at 6\u2005weeks, ED length of stay (EDLOS), straight leg raise (SLR) angles and Oswestry functional scores."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Patients treated with dexamethasone had a 1.86 point (95% CI 0.31 to 3.42, p=0.019) greater reduction in VAS pain scores at 24\u2005h than placebo (dexamethasone: -2.63 (95% CI -3.63 to -1.63) versus placebo: -0.77 (95% CI -2.04 to 0.51)). At 6\u2005weeks, both groups had similar significant and sustained decrease in VAS scores compared with baseline. Patients receiving dexamethasone had a significantly shorter EDLOS (median: 3.5\u2005h vs 18.8\u2005h, p=0.049) and improved SLR angle at discharge (14.7°, p=0.040). There was no difference in functional scores."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "In patients with LBPR, a single dose of intravenous dexamethasone in addition to routine management improved VAS pain scores at 24\u2005h, but this effect was not statistically significant at 6\u2005weeks. Dexamethasone may reduce EDLOS and can be considered as a safe adjunct to standard treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION NUMBER",
            "content": "ACTRN12611001020976."
          }
        ]
      },
